{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "interaction_emergence",
      "parent_relation": "For fixed zeta, eta changes the dispersion through a single shared amplitude factor multiplying a fixed kappa-shape correction, so the eta-dependence is effectively separable from the wavevector dependence apart from that preset saturation form.",
      "child_relation": "Let the environment control not only the magnitude but also the saturation/rolloff of the nonlocal correction, so the kappa-shape of the dispersion changes with eta through an eta-dependent weakening scale.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "kappa",
        "eta",
        "zeta"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "As eta is varied at fixed zeta, the curvature and approach to the zone-boundary peak change non-self-similarly: the mid-zone stiffening can grow while the near-boundary correction saturates earlier or later, which a single parent parameter set with fixed zeta cannot match across multiple eta conditions."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "s2",
        "expression": "sin(kappa/2)**2",
        "role": "basic lattice phase measure controlling acoustic-to-zone-edge progression"
      },
      {
        "name": "env_nonlocal",
        "expression": "eta*sin(kappa/2)**2/(1 + zeta*sin(kappa/2)**2)",
        "role": "current environment-induced nonlocal harmonic correction strength"
      },
      {
        "name": "mid_vs_edge",
        "expression": "sin(kappa/2)**2/(1 + sin(kappa/2)**2)",
        "role": "coordinate distinguishing intermediate-zone curvature from zone-edge behavior"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Visible when eta spans values large enough that the nonlocal correction is not perturbatively tiny, and when kappa samples both long-wavelength and mid/upper-zone regions; if eta≈0 or only very small |kappa| are observed, the difference is weak.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Within training, varying eta already changes the dispersion amplitude; in extended holdout at larger eta or denser mid-zone sampling, the additional eta-driven change in saturation scale produces shape changes across kappa that extrapolate the same mechanism.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent by taking the eta-dependence of the saturation scale to zero, so the weakening denominator returns to a fixed zeta-controlled form.",
    "freedom_change": "parameter_to_input",
    "promoted_symbol": "zeta",
    "promotion_reason": "A fixed saturation parameter forces one common wavevector-shape of the environment correction for all environments. To model condition-dependent reshaping of the dispersion, the saturation control itself must vary across experiments rather than remain globally fixed.",
    "gap_statement": "The current law assumes the environment changes only the strength of effective further-neighbor coupling, while the wavevector scale at which that correction weakens is held identical across all environments.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The saturation parameter zeta is a fixed material constant independent of the external environment control eta.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Environment-dependent screening or spatial decay of effective nonlocal harmonic interactions changes both their low-k strength and the lattice-phase scale over which they saturate.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "kappa",
      "eta",
      "zeta"
    ],
    "rationale": "Parent response relationship: Omega is determined by |sin(kappa/2)| times a square-root correction in which eta tunes the size of a nonlocal harmonic contribution, while zeta fixes one shared rolloff versus lattice phase. Changed target mapping: release the assumption that this rolloff is universal, so changing the environment can also reshape where along kappa the correction bends over. Varied condition: eta (and, after promotion, zeta across sampled environments); held fixed: lattice spacing, nearest-neighbor reference stiffness, monatomic structure, and the measured kappa grid. Observable contrast: compare low-to-intermediate kappa curvature and the approach to the zone-boundary peak for two environment settings. The parent with one shared refitted zeta can only scale a fixed correction shape with eta, so it cannot reproduce cases where one environment shows strong mid-zone enhancement but early saturation near the edge while another shows a broader unsaturated enhancement across the zone. This is a genuine change in the scored mapping Omega(kappa, environment), not merely an auxiliary reinterpretation. Admissible activation requires eta not be negligible and data include enough of the Brillouin zone to separate curvature change from simple gain change; otherwise the refinement may be only weakly identifiable."
  },
  "operation": "change_assumption",
